/* Pre-computed projection mixing and demixing matrices for 6th- to 14th-order
 * ambisonics. The 1st- to 5th-order matrices live in mapping_matrix.c.
 *
 * These are built differently from the 4th- and 5th-order tables. Those sample
 * the spherical-harmonic matrix at Fliege-Maier cubature nodes and invert it in
 * the least-squares sense, which is well conditioned up to 9th order and then
 * degrades badly (cond(A) = 86 at 10th order, 723 at 12th) because those node
 * sets are chosen to be good cubature rules, not good interpolation points.
 * A poorly conditioned mixing matrix costs SNR: per-sector coding noise comes
 * back ||A||_F.||A^-1||_F/N times louder in the ambisonic domain, which for the
 * naive extension is 15 dB at 10th order and 32 dB at 12th.
 *
 * Instead these sample at maximum-determinant ("Fekete") nodes, which come in
 * sets of exactly (N+1)^2 points chosen to maximize |det Y| -- i.e. to optimize
 * the very interpolation matrix in question -- and then replace the result by
 * its polar factor U.V^T, the nearest matrix with orthonormal columns. That is
 * energy-preserving ambisonic decoding (Zotter, Pomberger & Noisternig, Acta
 * Acustica 98(1), 2012) at the critically sampled limit L = (N+1)^2. It gives,
 * at every order:
 *
 *   - cond(A) = 1, so the demixing step amplifies coding noise by 0 dB;
 *   - no gain field, since neither matrix ever exceeds unity magnitude;
 *   - a demixing matrix that is the exact transpose of the mixing matrix, so
 *     quantizing one and transposing gives bit-identically the other;
 *   - a Q15 round trip whose error stays at the quantization floor (~1e-4)
 *     rather than growing with order.
 *
 * Generated with AmbisonicArrays.jl:
 *   write_opus_mapping_matrices("mapping_matrix_hoa.c", opus_mapping_matrices.(6:14))
 */

#ifdef HAVE_CONFIG_H
#include "config.h"
#endif

#include "arch.h"
#include "mapping_matrix.h"

